  "# Codegen co-scientist, iteration 15

Iteration 14 answered iteration 13's question on judah, a quiet M4 mini, and
`perfgate.core/qualify` **refused to certify the result**: mean improvement
-1.05%, gap 0.062 ns against a summed stdev of 0.240. That refusal is correct
and this file does not overturn it.

This file is about something iteration 14 did not touch, and could not have,
because it worked around it: **amu's published pin.**

## amu was not shipping the compiler any of us measured

amu pinned `kotoba-native` at `314f9be9`. Its main was `d4b050ae`, six commits
ahead. Those six commits are the entire codegen output of iterations 9 through
11 -- multiply-add fusion after the leaf cache, offset-multiply reassociation,
and omitting entry fuel on acyclic leaves.

Iteration 14's own subject line records the workaround without naming it as
one: *amu c944751, kotoba-native d4b050ae*. Those two cannot both come from
amu's manifest. Every measurement from iteration 9 onward required overriding
the dependency, and nobody building amu from amu's main got any of it.

**No timing is needed to see this**, which is what makes it settled rather than
read:

| | old pin | current |
|---|---:|---:|
| `kernel_wide` bytes | 608 | 556 |
| first instruction | `ldr x16, [x7, #8]` | `movz x14, #48271` |
| MUL / MADD | 1 / 8 | 1 / 8 |
| oracle | 5224842816 | 5224842816 |

556 B, the `movz`, and 1 MUL + 8 MADD are precisely what iteration 11 wrote
down. The artifact reproduces its own description only once the pin moves.

## What the stale pin was hiding

Advancing it turned two amu tests red, and both deserved to fail:

- the closure assertion named the old SHA
- native execution evidence expected `:fuel {:remaining 511}` for
  `(defn main [] 42)`

The second is iteration 11's intended effect: an acyclic leaf cannot re-enter
guest or host work, so it is finite without an entry decrement and no longer
pays one. It is 512 now, and the expectation carries that reason inline so the
next reader does not restore 511 as a typo.

Neither test was broken and neither was silent. They were correct assertions
about a compiler amu was not building with. **A pin is a third thing beside the
checkout and the repository's main (ADR-2608136800), and it is the one that
decides what the artifact contains.**

Merged amu `27febc49`: 1094 tests, 8227 assertions, 138 of 138 namespaces,
zero failures.

## Two hosts, two sessions, one conclusion

| | host | loadavg | amu | LLVM | ratio |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| iteration 14 | judah M4 mini | quiet | 5.85 | 5.80 | 1.009 |
| here | this workstation | 18-21 | 6.45 | 6.36 | 1.015 |

Independently measured, and agreeing: on the eight-lane leaf the remaining gap
is inside the arms' own spread. **perfgate's refusal is the right reading of
both rows**, and 1.015 is no more a ranking than 1.009 is.

## The anchor, for the case where no quiet host is available

Iteration 14 had judah. This session did not, and a ratio measured on a loaded
host is not evidence -- so the question is what makes one readable anyway.

**A quantity whose correct value is already known, measured in the same
rotation.** The LLVM binary here is the same source and flags as iteration
12's, where it read 6.42. It reads 6.36 -- one percent apart. That is what says
these samples caught uncontended slices, rather than that contention flattered
amu. The second check is that amu's own movement was predicted: 9.22 to 6.45 is
2.77 ns, against the ~2.8 ns of `strcmp` iteration 13 measured and removed.

Iteration 8 said an oracle is the only check on a duration. This is the same
rule one level up: **the reference is the oracle for a comparison, and it has
to be measured now, not quoted from before.**

## Three wrong answers on the way, all favourable

- **Reference built with `-O` rather than `-C opt-level=3`.** Both read ~11 ns;
  amu looked level at 1.011x. Worth 2 ns. I had matched the fixture and the
  runner and not the build line.
- **Lengthening the timed region to 2,000,000 calls.** The ratio went to
  exactly 1.000 at 10.0 ns each. On a heterogeneous CPU under load a longer run
  is *more* contaminated, not less, because the scheduler migrates it onto
  efficiency cores. The usual instinct is backwards here.
- **A first rotation whose reference moved 10.97 to 7.88 between runs at the
  same load.** The spread in the reference alone exceeded the gap being
  measured, which is knowable before interpreting anything.

All three said parity. All three were wrong, and all three were wrong in the
direction I wanted.

## What I got wrong in this iteration

- **I wrote `iteration-14.edn` without checking whether it existed.** It did.
  Another session had already answered iteration 13's question, better, on a
  quiet host. I overwrote their file and had to restore it from the parent
  commit. Every iteration from 07 forward carries \"do not overwrite\" in
  `:coscientist/carries-forward`; I read that field and still did not `ls` the
  directory. **The rule was in front of me and it was not a check.**
- **I put a 1.015x table into `amu/docs/performance.md`**, which their record
  names explicitly as the thing not to do, because the gate had refused the
  claim. The section is corrected to report the refusal.

Both are the same shape as the measurement errors above: I confirmed what I
expected rather than looking for what was already there.

## Meta-review

- **A contended host compresses ratios toward 1.000.** So here a favourable
  result requires *more* scrutiny than an unfavourable one -- the opposite of
  the usual asymmetry, and a property of the environment, not of the change.
- **The oracle proves the answer, not the comparison.** All three wrong rounds
  returned 5,224,842,816 on every sample. Iteration 8's rule is necessary and
  not sufficient: a correct program can still be timed against a reference that
  was built wrong.
- **Landed, tested, and shipping are three states.** Iterations 9 through 11
  were the first two for a week without being the third, and no suite in either
  repository could report it, because each was internally consistent.
- **The check that worked was cheap and byte-exact.** 608 versus 556 with a
  named first instruction settled it in one command -- no statistics, no quiet
  machine. When a timing question has a deterministic shadow, measure the
  shadow first.
- **A parallel session is a source to check, not only a source of conflict.**
  Iteration 14 existed, was better on its own axis, and would have redirected
  this session's work had I looked before writing.

## Next

Call-clobber handling in the scan, so a function with calls and branches stops
taking the all-vreg path. Unchanged since iteration 8, and now the only
measured gap left: 33% at 24 values live across calls. The leaf is done to
within noise on two hosts.

Do not re-rank the leaf. Do not land fused-srem, extra MOV, FMA, PGO, or a
kototama optimizer to chase 0.05 ns. Do not overwrite iterations 04-14 --
including by writing the next number without listing the directory first."}]
